            {
              "id": "416deb8c-c59a-4d4e-9197-220746caf880",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "html": "<em><strong>Games of Empire: Global Capitalism &amp; Video Games</strong></em> was written by Nick Dyer-Witherford &amp; Greig de Peuter, published by University Of Minnesota Press, December 8, 2009."
            },
            {
              "id": "07614ec4-32b9-45bd-90d8-65cc2b4eaa58",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "html": ""
            },
            {
              "id": "5ccbbb32-a421-42c4-a11b-55e6a0368414",
              "type": "quote",
              "html": "<em>In the first decade of the twenty-first century, video games are an integral part of global media culture, rivaling Hollywood in revenue and influence. No longer confined to a subculture of adolescent males, video games today are played by adults around the world. At the same time, video games have become major sites of corporate exploitation and military recruitment.<br></em>"
            },
            {
              "id": "0c5dd5b3-ee8c-4e4b-bdff-65f2058af83c",
